const LESSONS = {
/* ============================== COHESION ============================== */
"coh.reference": {
  name:"Referring back clearly", tech:"Reference",
  short:"Use it, they, this and these so readers know exactly what you mean.",
  ex:"The university introduced a new policy. This policy reduced fees.",
  def:"Reference words (it, they, this, these, that, those, such) point back to something you have already mentioned. The reader must be able to find that thing, called the antecedent, quickly and without doubt.",
  why:"Academic paragraphs carry several ideas at once. If a reader cannot tell what \"it\" or \"this\" refers to, the argument breaks, even when the grammar is correct. Using a pronoun does not automatically create good cohesion.",
  good:"Many universities now record lectures. This practice allows students to review difficult content.",
  bad:"Universities record lectures and students use online forums. This has improved learning.",
  badWhy:"\"This\" could mean recording lectures, using forums, or both. The reader has to guess.",
  strategy:["Underline every it, they, this and these in your paragraph.","Draw an arrow to what each one refers to. If there are two possible targets, it is ambiguous.","Add a summary noun after this or these (this practice, these changes), or repeat the noun.","Check number: it and this for one thing, they and these for more than one."],
  worked:{before:"The government funded the project and the university supplied the staff. However, it was not enough.",
    steps:["\"It\" could refer to the funding, the staff or the project.","From the context, the writer means the funding.","Replace \"it\" with a clear noun phrase."],
    after:"The government funded the project and the university supplied the staff. However, this funding was not enough."},
  check:{q:"\"Researchers surveyed 200 nurses and 50 doctors. They reported high levels of stress.\" What is the problem with \"They\"?",
    opts:["Nothing. It clearly means the researchers.","It could mean the nurses, the doctors, both groups or the researchers.","It should be \"It\".","\"They\" cannot start a sentence."],
    a:1, hint:"Count how many plural groups come before \"They\".",
    why:"Three plural groups come before \"They\", so the reader cannot be sure which one reported stress. A noun such as \"Both groups\" or \"The nurses\" fixes it."},
  diag:{a:"Online learning is flexible and cheap. These advantages make it popular with working students.", b:"Online learning is flexible, and many students also work part-time. This makes it popular."},
  task:{instr:"Revise the paragraph so that every reference word has one clear meaning. Change only what you need to.",
    text:"Social media allows students to share resources, and lecturers can post updates quickly. However, it can also distract them. This is why some universities limit its use in class."},
  indep:{instr:"Revise the paragraph so that every reference word has one clear meaning.",
    text:"The council built a new library and extended bus services to the area. They were popular with residents, but it was expensive. This led to a rise in local taxes."},
  criteria:["Every reference word has one clear antecedent","Ambiguous words are replaced or followed by a summary noun","Singular and plural forms agree","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"In your next paragraph, underline every it, they, this and these. Check that each one points to exactly one thing a reader can find."
},
"coh.conjunction": {
  name:"Linking ideas with connectors", tech:"Conjunction",
  short:"Show the relationship between ideas: addition, contrast, cause and result.",
  ex:"The method was effective. However, it was expensive.",
  def:"Conjunctions and linking adverbials (however, therefore, in addition, because, although) signal how one idea relates to another: addition, contrast, cause, result, sequence or condition.",
  why:"A linker tells the reader how to read the next idea. The wrong linker sends the reader in the wrong direction, and too many linkers make writing sound mechanical.",
  good:"The new timetable reduced waiting times. As a result, student satisfaction increased.",
  bad:"The new timetable reduced waiting times. However, student satisfaction increased.",
  badWhy:"\"However\" signals contrast, but the second idea is a result of the first, not a contrast.",
  strategy:["Decide the relationship first: addition, contrast, cause, result, sequence or condition.","Choose a linker for that relationship (in addition / however, although / because, since / therefore, as a result / first, then / if, unless).","Check punctuation: \"However,\" and \"Therefore,\" usually start a sentence or follow a semicolon, with a comma after them.","Remove linkers where the relationship is already obvious."],
  worked:{before:"Many students work part-time. Moreover, they have less time to study.",
    steps:["The second sentence is a consequence of the first, not an extra point.","\"Moreover\" signals addition, so it shows the wrong relationship.","Use a result linker instead."],
    after:"Many students work part-time. As a result, they have less time to study."},
  check:{q:"Choose the best linker: \"The sample was small. ___, the results should be treated with caution.\"",
    opts:["However","Therefore","In addition","Although"], a:1, hint:"Is the caution a contrast, an extra point or a consequence of the small sample?",
    why:"The caution is a result of the small sample, so a result linker fits. \"Although\" also cannot join two full sentences in this way."},
  diag:{a:"The drug reduced pain. However, it caused side effects in some patients.", b:"The drug reduced pain. Therefore, it caused side effects in some patients."},
  task:{instr:"Some linkers in this paragraph show the wrong relationship, and some are not needed. Revise them.",
    text:"Remote work saves commuting time. Furthermore, some employees feel isolated. Therefore, many companies now offer a mix of office and home days. Moreover, this approach may not suit every role."},
  indep:{instr:"Some linkers in this paragraph show the wrong relationship, and some are not needed. Revise them.",
    text:"Public transport reduces traffic. However, it also lowers emissions. Moreover, many cities have increased fares. Therefore, fewer people now use buses. In addition, governments should review their pricing."},
  criteria:["Each linker matches the real relationship between the ideas","Unnecessary linkers are removed","Punctuation with linking adverbials is correct","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"Before you submit your next paragraph, circle each linker and write the relationship (contrast, result, addition) above it. If the label does not fit, change the linker."
},
"coh.substitution": {
  name:"Replacing repeated words", tech:"Substitution",
  short:"Use one, ones, do or so instead of repeating the same phrase.",
  ex:"Some students prefer printed books; others prefer digital ones.",
  def:"Substitution replaces a word or phrase with a short substitute so you do not repeat it: one or ones for countable nouns, do, does or did for verb phrases, and so or not for whole clauses (I think so, I hope not).",
  why:"Repeating long phrases makes writing heavy. Substitution keeps it concise, but it only works when the reader can tell exactly what has been replaced.",
  good:"Older laboratories lack ventilation, but the new ones meet safety standards.",
  bad:"Older laboratories lack ventilation, but the new laboratories meet safety standards. The old equipment is unsafe, so the faculty will buy a new one.",
  badWhy:"\"Laboratories\" is repeated where \"ones\" would work. \"Equipment\" is uncountable, so \"a new one\" is incorrect: write \"new equipment\".",
  strategy:["Look for a noun or verb phrase repeated in neighbouring clauses.","Replace a countable noun with one (singular) or ones (plural). Do not use one for uncountable nouns.","Replace a repeated verb phrase with do, does or did (or \"do so\" in formal writing).","Check that the substitute can only refer to one thing."],
  worked:{before:"The first survey produced reliable data, but the second survey did not produce reliable data.",
    steps:["\"survey\" and \"produce reliable data\" are both repeated.","\"Survey\" is countable, so \"one\" can replace it.","The verb phrase can be replaced by \"did not\"."],
    after:"The first survey produced reliable data, but the second one did not."},
  check:{q:"Which sentence uses substitution correctly?",
    opts:["The old microscope was faulty, so the lab bought a new one.","The old information was outdated, so the team found a new one.","The old policies were unfair, so the council wrote a new ones.","The old method failed, so the team did it one again."], a:0,
    hint:"Check whether each replaced noun is countable, and whether one or ones matches the number.",
    why:"\"Microscope\" is a countable singular noun, so \"a new one\" works. \"Information\" is uncountable, and \"a new ones\" mixes singular and plural."},
  diag:{a:"Some cities have built new stadiums, while others have renovated old ones.", b:"Some cities have built new stadiums, while other cities have renovated old stadiums."},
  task:{instr:"Reduce the unnecessary repetition using substitution (one, ones, do, does, did, so). Keep the meaning clear.",
    text:"Large universities offer more courses than small universities offer. Some students prefer large universities, while other students prefer small universities. Students who live on campus usually make friends faster than students who do not live on campus."},
  indep:{instr:"Reduce the unnecessary repetition using substitution. Keep the meaning clear.",
    text:"The first proposal was cheaper than the second proposal. The committee rejected the first proposal, but the committee approved the second proposal because the second proposal included a training plan."},
  criteria:["Unnecessary repetition is reduced","One and ones are used only for countable nouns, with the correct number","Do, does, did, so or not are used correctly","Every substitute is clear and the meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"When you revise, look for the same noun or verb phrase used twice in one sentence. Ask whether one, ones or did could replace it without confusing the reader."
},
"coh.ellipsis": {
  name:"Leaving out what is already clear", tech:"Ellipsis",
  short:"Leave out repeated words when the reader can easily recover them.",
  ex:"Some students chose economics, while others [chose] engineering.",
  def:"Ellipsis means leaving out words that the reader can easily recover from the previous clause, such as a repeated verb or noun.",
  why:"It avoids heavy repetition and makes comparisons tighter. But if the reader cannot recover the missing words, the sentence becomes unclear or ungrammatical.",
  good:"The first group improved by 12%, and the second by 8%.",
  bad:"Participants in the morning session completed the test faster than the afternoon.",
  badWhy:"Too much is left out: \"the afternoon\" did not complete a test. The sentence needs \"than those in the afternoon session\".",
  strategy:["Find a repeated verb or noun in two parallel clauses.","Remove the repeated words only from the second clause.","Read the second clause and check you can recover exactly what is missing.","If the omission compares unlike things or leaves a gap, put words back."],
  worked:{before:"Group A received the new treatment and Group B received a placebo.",
    steps:["\"received\" is repeated in two parallel clauses.","The reader can easily recover it from the first clause.","Remove it from the second clause and add a comma."],
    after:"Group A received the new treatment, and Group B a placebo."},
  check:{q:"Which sentence uses ellipsis successfully?",
    opts:["Prices rose by 5% in 2022 and by 7% in 2023.","Prices rose by 5% in 2022 and wages.","Urban students scored higher than rural.","The report was published and the data."], a:0,
    hint:"In which sentence can you say exactly which words are missing?",
    why:"Only \"rose\" is left out, and the reader recovers it easily. The other sentences leave out too much, so the meaning is incomplete."},
  diag:{a:"Students in the online course completed more tasks than those in the classroom course.", b:"Students in the online course completed more tasks than the classroom."},
  task:{instr:"Some sentences repeat words unnecessarily; others leave out too much. Revise them.",
    text:"In 2010, 40% of graduates found work within six months. In 2020, 65% of graduates found work within six months. Graduates from business courses earned more than arts. Some graduates moved overseas and some graduates moved to regional areas."},
  indep:{instr:"Some sentences repeat words unnecessarily; others leave out too much. Revise them.",
    text:"The first experiment took three weeks and the second experiment took five weeks. Participants in the first experiment reported less fatigue than the second. Some participants withdrew early and some participants completed every session."},
  criteria:["Unnecessary repetition is removed where the missing words can be recovered","Comparisons keep enough words to compare like with like","Sentences stay grammatical and clear","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"When you compare two groups or years, check that the reader can recover anything you leave out, and that you compare like with like."
},
"coh.lexical": {
  name:"Keeping the topic with related words", tech:"Lexical cohesion",
  short:"Repeat key terms and use related vocabulary to hold the topic together.",
  ex:"The study examined student motivation. The research also considered engagement.",
  def:"Lexical cohesion is created through vocabulary: repeating key terms, using accurate synonyms, moving between general and specific words (transport → buses) and using words from the same topic field.",
  why:"Readers follow a topic through its vocabulary. Random synonyms confuse them (are \"learners\" a different group from \"students\"?), while endless repetition sounds heavy.",
  good:"Air pollution is rising in large cities. Vehicle emissions are the main source of this pollution, especially from older diesel cars.",
  bad:"Air pollution is rising in large cities. Atmospheric dirtiness mainly comes from transport smoke.",
  badWhy:"\"Atmospheric dirtiness\" and \"transport smoke\" are inaccurate synonyms. Keep the key term \"pollution\" and use precise related words such as \"vehicle emissions\".",
  strategy:["Identify your key term and keep it. Technical terms usually should not be swapped for synonyms.","Use a synonym only if it means the same thing in this context.","Use general and specific pairs (transport → buses and trains) to move the topic forward.","Check that new words belong to the same topic field."],
  worked:{before:"Childhood obesity is increasing. Kid fatness is linked to diet. Young people heaviness also relates to screen time.",
    steps:["\"Kid fatness\" and \"young people heaviness\" are informal and inaccurate.","\"Childhood obesity\" is the key term: repeat it or use \"this condition\".","Add precise words from the same field: diet, physical activity, screen time."],
    after:"Childhood obesity is increasing. This condition is closely linked to diet, and childhood obesity is also associated with increased screen time."},
  check:{q:"In a paragraph about \"renewable energy\", which phrase is the best way to refer back to it?",
    opts:["green power stuff","clean energy sources","nice energy","alternative fuels for cars"], a:1,
    hint:"Look for a phrase that is formal and means the same thing, not something narrower.",
    why:"\"Clean energy sources\" is close in meaning and formal. \"Alternative fuels for cars\" is narrower, so it shifts the topic."},
  diag:{a:"The survey measured job satisfaction. Satisfaction was highest among part-time staff.", b:"The survey measured job satisfaction. Work happiness was highest among part-time staff."},
  task:{instr:"Improve the vocabulary links so the topic stays clear and precise.",
    text:"Deforestation is a serious problem in many tropical countries. Tree cutting reduces habitats for animals. Wood removal also increases carbon in the air. Governments are trying to stop the forest thing."},
  indep:{instr:"Improve the vocabulary links so the topic stays clear and precise.",
    text:"Unemployment among young people has increased. Youth joblessness affects mental health. Not having jobs also reduces tax revenue. Governments need to solve the job situation for teenagers."},
  criteria:["The key term is used consistently","Synonyms are accurate and formal","Related words develop the topic","The meaning and topic do not change"],
  transfer:"Highlight your key term in each sentence of your next paragraph. Where you used a synonym, check that it means exactly the same thing."
},
"coh.flow": {
  name:"Linking each sentence to the last", tech:"Given–new information flow",
  short:"Start sentences with known information and end them with new information.",
  ex:"…a new vaccine. This vaccine was tested on 2,000 volunteers.",
  def:"English sentences usually begin with information the reader already knows (given) and end with new information. The new information at the end of one sentence often becomes the starting point of the next.",
  why:"When each sentence picks up from the one before, the paragraph reads smoothly without needing many linking words.",
  good:"Researchers developed a new water filter. The filter uses plant fibres, which are cheap and widely available.",
  bad:"Researchers developed a new water filter. Cheap and widely available plant fibres are used by the filter.",
  badWhy:"The second sentence starts with brand-new information, so the reader loses the link with \"filter\".",
  strategy:["Look at the start of each sentence: does it connect to something in the previous sentence?","Move known information to the front, often by changing word order or using the passive.","Put new or important information at the end.","Use a reference phrase (this filter, these results) to make the starting point clear."],
  worked:{before:"The city opened a new cycle lane in 2021. A 30% fall in cyclist injuries followed its opening.",
    steps:["Sentence 2 starts with new information (\"A 30% fall…\").","The given information is the lane and its opening.","Start with the lane and end with the new result."],
    after:"The city opened a new cycle lane in 2021. In the year after the lane opened, cyclist injuries fell by 30%."},
  check:{q:"Which second sentence follows best? \"The study focused on first-year students.\"",
    opts:["These students completed a weekly online survey.","A weekly online survey was completed by them.","Weekly online surveys are useful in many fields.","Completion of a weekly online survey was required."], a:0,
    hint:"Which option starts with something the first sentence already mentioned?",
    why:"It starts with given information (\"These students\") and ends with the new information (the survey)."},
  diag:{a:"The team designed a new app. The app helps students track their deadlines.", b:"The team designed a new app. Deadline tracking for students is the purpose of the app."},
  task:{instr:"Reorder information inside the sentences so that each one starts from something the reader already knows.",
    text:"Many cities have introduced congestion charges. A significant reduction in traffic was produced by these charges in London. Increased use of public transport also resulted from the reduction."},
  indep:{instr:"Reorder information inside the sentences so that each one starts from something the reader already knows.",
    text:"The university introduced a peer mentoring program in 2022. Higher first-year retention was reported after the program. Training for mentors is provided by senior students."},
  criteria:["Each sentence starts with given or linked information","New information is placed towards the end","Grammar stays accurate, including any passive forms","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"Read only the first few words of each sentence in your next paragraph. Each one should connect to something already mentioned."
},
"coh.coherence": {
  name:"Making ideas progress logically", tech:"Coherence and logical progression",
  short:"Order your ideas so the paragraph makes sense as a whole.",
  ex:"A problem is introduced, its cause explained, then its consequences discussed.",
  def:"Coherence is the logical sense of a text: one clear focus, ideas in a sensible order, and every sentence relevant to the main point. Cohesion is about the language links between sentences. A paragraph can have many linkers and still be incoherent.",
  why:"Readers need to see where the paragraph is going. If ideas jump around or include irrelevant points, the argument is hard to follow, even when every sentence is correct.",
  good:"Food waste is a growing problem in households. A major cause is poor meal planning, which leads people to buy more than they need. As a result, large amounts of edible food are thrown away, increasing landfill emissions.",
  bad:"Food waste is a growing problem in households. Landfill emissions are increasing. Supermarkets now sell imperfect vegetables. People often buy more than they need.",
  badWhy:"The ideas are related, but the order is random: an effect, an unrelated point, then the cause. There is no clear line from problem to cause to consequence.",
  strategy:["Write the paragraph's main point in one sentence.","Choose an order that suits it: problem → cause → effect, claim → reason → example, or general → specific.","Check each sentence: does it support the main point? Move or remove it if not.","Then add cohesive links (linkers, reference words) to show that order."],
  worked:{before:"Online exams are increasingly common. Students can take them from home. Some students cheat by using notes. Universities now use proctoring software. Exams are also cheaper to run online.",
    steps:["The main point is unclear: is it about benefits or about integrity?","Choose one focus: the risk to academic integrity.","Order it as development → problem → response, and move the cost point to another paragraph."],
    after:"Online exams are increasingly common, but they create new risks to academic integrity. Because students sit them at home, some use notes or other unauthorised help. In response, many universities now use proctoring software to monitor exams."},
  check:{q:"Which statement about cohesion and coherence is accurate?",
    opts:["They mean the same thing.","Adding more linking words always makes a paragraph coherent.","Coherence is about logical sense and order; cohesion is about language links between sentences.","Coherence is only about grammar."], a:2,
    hint:"One of them is about ideas and their order; the other is about the words that link sentences.",
    why:"Coherence is about whether ideas make sense and progress logically. Cohesion is the language that links sentences. Good writing needs both."},
  diag:{a:"Exercise improves mental health. It reduces stress hormones, which lowers anxiety. For this reason, some doctors now prescribe exercise.", b:"Exercise improves mental health. Some doctors now prescribe it. Gyms can be expensive. It reduces stress hormones."},
  task:{instr:"Reorganise this paragraph so that it has one clear focus and a logical order. You may move or remove sentences and add short links.",
    text:"International students bring many benefits to universities. Housing costs in cities have risen. Students from different countries share new perspectives in class. Universities also earn significant income from their fees. Some students find the weather difficult."},
  indep:{instr:"Reorganise this paragraph so that it has one clear focus and a logical order. You may move or remove sentences and add short links.",
    text:"Plastic bags harm marine life. Many countries have introduced bag bans. Paper bags need more energy to produce. Animals often mistake plastic for food. Bans have reduced plastic litter on beaches in several countries."},
  criteria:["The paragraph has one clear focus","Ideas follow a logical order","Irrelevant sentences are removed or moved","Links show how the ideas connect"],
  transfer:"Before writing your next paragraph, note its main point and the order of your ideas (for example, problem → cause → effect). Check the draft against that plan."
},

/* ============================== PARAPHRASING ============================== */
"para.meaning": {
  name:"Understanding the source first", tech:"Understanding source meaning",
  short:"Understand the whole idea of a source before you try to rewrite it.",
  ex:"Read, look away, explain it to yourself, then write.",
  def:"A paraphrase restates someone else's idea in your own words and structure, with the same meaning. You cannot do this until you fully understand the original, including who does what and how certain the claim is.",
  why:"Many weak paraphrases come from partial understanding. The writer changes words one by one and accidentally changes the meaning.",
  good:"Source: \"Regular sleep patterns appear to improve students' concentration during lectures.\"\nParaphrase: Students who keep a consistent sleep schedule seem to focus better in class.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: Students who sleep a lot always concentrate in lectures.",
  badWhy:"\"Regular\" (consistent) became \"a lot\" (amount), and \"appear to\" became \"always\". The meaning has changed.",
  strategy:["Read the source twice and check any word you are unsure of.","Answer: Who or what? Does what? Under what conditions? How certain is it?","Cover the source and explain the idea in notes or aloud.","Write from your notes, then compare with the source for meaning."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"Increases in tuition fees have led some low-income students to delay enrolling at university.\"",
    steps:["Who: some low-income students. What: delay enrolling. Why: higher fees. Certainty: \"some\".","Notes: higher fees → some poorer students start university later.","Write from the notes, keeping \"some\"."],
    after:"Paraphrase: Rising university fees have caused a number of students from low-income backgrounds to postpone starting their studies."},
  check:{q:"Source: \"Most participants preferred the online version of the course.\" Which paraphrase keeps the meaning?",
    opts:["All participants liked the online course.","The majority of those in the study favoured the online form of the course.","Participants said the online course was the most effective.","Most participants completed the course online."], a:1,
    hint:"Check two things: how many people (most) and what they did (preferred).",
    why:"\"The majority\" keeps \"most\", and \"favoured\" keeps \"preferred\". The others change how many people, or what they did."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Some studies suggest coffee may reduce the risk of diabetes.\" → Research indicates that coffee might lower the likelihood of developing diabetes.", b:"Source: \"Some studies suggest coffee may reduce the risk of diabetes.\" → Studies prove that coffee prevents diabetes."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source. Keep the full meaning, including who, what and how certain.",
    text:"Source: \"Students who participate in extracurricular activities tend to report a stronger sense of belonging at university.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source. Keep the full meaning, including who, what and how certain.",
    text:"Source: \"Flexible working arrangements can help employees with caring responsibilities to remain in the workforce.\""},
  criteria:["All key ideas from the source are included","No information is added or distorted","The level of certainty is kept","Wording and structure are the student's own"],
  transfer:"Before paraphrasing, write three quick notes: who or what, does what, and how certain. Paraphrase from the notes, not from the source."
},
"para.keyinfo": {
  name:"Picking out the key information", tech:"Identifying key information",
  short:"Separate the main idea of a source from its supporting detail.",
  ex:"Main claim vs examples, dates and extra description.",
  def:"Before paraphrasing or summarising, identify the essential meaning (the main claim) and decide which details your own writing needs.",
  why:"If you try to keep every detail, you will end up copying the structure. If you drop the main claim, the paraphrase is inaccurate.",
  good:"Source: \"Urban green spaces, such as parks and community gardens, have been shown to reduce stress among city residents, particularly those living in high-density housing.\"\nKey idea: Green spaces reduce stress for city residents, especially in dense housing. The examples (parks, gardens) are optional.",
  bad:"Key idea: Parks and community gardens exist in cities.",
  badWhy:"This is only the example. The main claim (green spaces reduce stress) is missing.",
  strategy:["Find the main verb of the source: what is being claimed?","Mark examples (such as…), lists and extra description as detail.","Keep qualifications that change the meaning (particularly, some, may).","Decide which details your own paragraph needs."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"Despite higher initial costs, electric buses, which are now used in several European cities, can lower long-term operating expenses for councils.\"",
    steps:["Main claim: electric buses can lower councils' long-term operating costs.","Contrast to keep: they cost more at first.","Detail: used in European cities (optional)."],
    after:"Paraphrase: Although electric buses are more expensive to buy, they may reduce councils' running costs over time."},
  check:{q:"Source: \"Bilingual children, who are exposed to two languages from birth, often show stronger attention control than monolingual children.\" What is the main claim?",
    opts:["Bilingual children hear two languages from birth.","Bilingual children often have better attention control than monolingual children.","Monolingual children speak one language.","Children are exposed to languages from birth."], a:1,
    hint:"Find the main verb of the sentence. The part between commas is extra description.",
    why:"The main verb is \"show\": bilingual children often show stronger attention control. \"Who are exposed… from birth\" is supporting detail."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Online tutoring, delivered twice a week, improved maths results for struggling students.\" → Key idea: online tutoring helped struggling students do better in maths.", b:"Source: \"Online tutoring, delivered twice a week, improved maths results for struggling students.\" → Key idea: the tutoring happened twice a week."},
  task:{instr:"Write the key idea of the source in one short line. Then paraphrase it in a full sentence.",
    text:"Source: \"Short, frequent quizzes, which many lecturers now use at the start of tutorials, can improve long-term retention of course material more than a single end-of-semester exam.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Write the key idea of the source in one short line. Then paraphrase it in a full sentence.",
    text:"Source: \"Community radio stations, which are often run by volunteers, play an important role in sharing local emergency information during natural disasters.\""},
  criteria:["The main claim is identified correctly","Essential qualifications are kept","Optional detail is handled sensibly","The paraphrase is accurate and in the student's own words"],
  transfer:"When you read a source for an assignment, underline its main verb and bracket the examples. Paraphrase the main claim first."
},
"para.synonyms": {
  name:"Choosing accurate synonyms", tech:"Synonym selection",
  short:"Replace words only with words that mean the same in this context.",
  ex:"\"a sharp decline\" → \"a rapid fall\", not \"a pointed refusal\".",
  def:"A synonym must match the original in meaning, register and grammar in that particular sentence. Many dictionary synonyms are not interchangeable.",
  why:"Inaccurate synonyms change the meaning or produce unnatural phrases. Changing words alone is also not enough for a paraphrase: the structure must change too.",
  good:"Source: \"The policy had a significant impact on enrolment.\"\nParaphrase: The policy considerably affected the number of students enrolling.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: The policy had a meaningful crash on registration.",
  badWhy:"\"Crash\" is a synonym of \"impact\" only in a physical sense, and \"meaningful\" does not mean \"large\". The structure is also unchanged.",
  strategy:["Check the meaning of the word in this sentence, not in general.","Check collocation: does the new word sound natural with its neighbours?","Keep technical terms (carbon dioxide, inflation) unchanged.","Change grammar as well as words."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"The researchers observed a sharp decline in bee populations.\"",
    steps:["\"sharp\" here means sudden and large → rapid or steep.","\"decline\" → fall or decrease (not refusal, another meaning of decline).","\"bee\" is the topic term: keep it, and change the structure."],
    after:"Paraphrase: According to the researchers, the number of bees fell rapidly."},
  check:{q:"Which word can replace \"acquire\" in \"Children acquire language naturally\"?",
    opts:["purchase","obtain","develop","buy"], a:2,
    hint:"Here \"acquire\" is about learning gradually, not getting an object.",
    why:"In this sentence \"acquire\" means to learn or develop gradually. \"Purchase\", \"buy\" and \"obtain\" all suggest getting an object."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Unemployment rose dramatically.\" → Joblessness increased sharply.", b:"Source: \"Unemployment rose dramatically.\" → Unemployment stood up theatrically."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source using accurate synonyms and some change of structure. Keep technical terms.",
    text:"Source: \"Rising sea levels pose a serious threat to low-lying coastal communities.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source using accurate synonyms and some change of structure. Keep technical terms.",
    text:"Source: \"The new medication considerably reduced symptoms in elderly patients.\""},
  criteria:["Synonyms are accurate in this context","Collocations sound natural","Technical terms are kept","The structure also changes and the meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"When you use a thesaurus, check each synonym in a sentence example (a learner's dictionary helps) before you use it."
},
"para.wordform": {
  name:"Changing word forms", tech:"Word-form (word class) changes",
  short:"Turn verbs into nouns, adjectives into adverbs, and build a new sentence around them.",
  ex:"\"Prices increased rapidly\" → \"There was a rapid increase in prices\".",
  def:"Changing a word's class (verb → noun, adjective → adverb) lets you build a different sentence around the same meaning. Other words usually need to change too.",
  why:"It is one of the most reliable ways to change structure while keeping meaning, and it moves your writing towards an academic style.",
  good:"Source: \"Prices increased rapidly in 2023.\"\nParaphrase: There was a rapid increase in prices in 2023.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: Prices increasement was rapid in 2023.",
  badWhy:"\"Increasement\" is not a word, and the noun needs a pattern such as \"the increase in prices\". Check the form and the words that go with it.",
  strategy:["Find a key verb or adjective in the source.","Change its word class (analyse → analysis, effective → effectively, reduce → reduction).","Rebuild the sentence around the new form, adding articles and prepositions (a reduction in, the analysis of).","Check that the new word exists, in a dictionary if you are unsure."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"Participants were highly satisfied with the service.\"",
    steps:["\"satisfied\" (adjective) → \"satisfaction\" (noun).","Build around the noun: \"reported a high level of satisfaction with\"."],
    after:"Paraphrase: Participants reported a high level of satisfaction with the service."},
  check:{q:"Which is a correct noun-based paraphrase of \"The drug was tested thoroughly\"?",
    opts:["The drug's test was thorough.","The drug underwent thorough testing.","The drug had a thoroughly test.","There was testing of drug thoroughly."], a:1,
    hint:"Look for a real noun used with a matching adjective (not an adverb).",
    why:"\"Testing\" is the noun, \"thorough\" is the adjective that goes with it, and \"underwent\" fits the meaning. The others have form or article errors."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Sales declined sharply.\" → There was a sharp decline in sales.", b:"Source: \"Sales declined sharply.\" → There was a sharp declination of sales."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing at least one key word form and rebuilding the sentence.",
    text:"Source: \"The city significantly expanded its recycling program in 2021.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing at least one key word form and rebuilding the sentence.",
    text:"Source: \"Students who were taught collaboratively performed more successfully in the final assessment.\""},
  criteria:["At least one word-form change is used","The new form is a real word used correctly","The sentence is rebuilt grammatically","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"When a paraphrase feels too close to the source, pick its main verb and try turning it into a noun (or the reverse)."
},
"para.grammar": {
  name:"Restructuring the grammar", tech:"Grammatical restructuring",
  short:"Change the voice, subject or order of a sentence, not just its words.",
  ex:"\"Researchers interviewed 40 nurses\" → \"Forty nurses were interviewed for the study\".",
  def:"Grammatical restructuring changes how a sentence is built: active ↔ passive, a different subject, or a different order of information, while keeping the meaning.",
  why:"Changing only words while keeping the original sentence pattern is still too close to the source.",
  good:"Source: \"Researchers interviewed 40 nurses about workplace stress.\"\nParaphrase: Workplace stress was the focus of interviews with 40 nurses.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: Scholars questioned 40 nurses regarding job pressure.",
  badWhy:"Every word was swapped, but the pattern is identical (subject – verb – object – about…). It is still too close to the source.",
  strategy:["Try making a different noun the subject.","Try active ↔ passive where it suits the focus.","Move a phrase (time, place, purpose) to another position.","Check the meaning: who did what must stay the same."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"The government introduced the new tax in 2019 to reduce sugar consumption.\"",
    steps:["Make the tax the subject and use the passive.","Move the purpose to the front.","Change a few words: reduce → cut, consumption → how much sugar people eat."],
    after:"Paraphrase: To cut how much sugar people eat, a new tax was introduced by the government in 2019."},
  check:{q:"Source: \"Teachers gave students extra time for the exam.\" Which paraphrase changes the structure, not only the words?",
    opts:["Educators provided learners additional time for the test.","Students were given extra time to complete the exam.","Teachers gave pupils more time for the exam.","Teachers gave students extra time for the test."], a:1,
    hint:"Which option has a different subject at the start?",
    why:"\"Students were given…\" uses a new subject and the passive. The other options keep the same pattern and only swap words."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Scientists discovered the virus in 1983.\" → The virus was first identified in 1983.", b:"Source: \"Scientists discovered the virus in 1983.\" → Researchers found the virus in 1983."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing its grammatical structure (subject, voice or order) as well as some words.",
    text:"Source: \"Local councils planted 10,000 trees last year to improve air quality.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing its grammatical structure (subject, voice or order) as well as some words.",
    text:"Source: \"The hospital trained all nursing staff in the new software before the system went live.\""},
  criteria:["The sentence structure is clearly different from the source","The meaning (who did what) is kept","Grammar is accurate","Some vocabulary is also changed appropriately"],
  transfer:"After writing a paraphrase, compare the first three words with the source. If they follow the same pattern, try a different subject or voice."
},
"para.clause": {
  name:"Restructuring clauses", tech:"Clause restructuring",
  short:"Combine, split or reorder clauses while keeping the same relationship between ideas.",
  ex:"\"X happened. As a result, Y.\" → \"Y because X.\"",
  def:"Clause restructuring changes how ideas are connected inside a sentence: two sentences become one, a main clause becomes a dependent clause, or a clause becomes a phrase (although it was expensive → despite its high cost).",
  why:"It shows the same relationship (cause, contrast, time) in a different form, which moves your paraphrase further from the source.",
  good:"Source: \"Although the program was expensive, it improved attendance.\"\nParaphrase: The program improved attendance, despite its high cost.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: Because the program was expensive, it improved attendance.",
  badWhy:"The relationship changed from contrast to cause, so the meaning is now wrong.",
  strategy:["Identify the relationship between the clauses (cause, contrast, time, condition).","Express it in a different form: although → despite + noun; because → due to + noun; two sentences → one.","Check that the relationship has not changed.","Check the punctuation and grammar of the new structure."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"The bridge was closed for repairs. As a result, traffic increased on nearby roads.\"",
    steps:["Relationship: cause → result.","Combine into one sentence with \"because\".","Put the result first."],
    after:"Paraphrase: Traffic on surrounding roads rose because the bridge had been closed for maintenance."},
  check:{q:"Which keeps the meaning of \"Despite strong demand, the company reduced production\"?",
    opts:["Because demand was strong, the company reduced production.","Although demand was high, the company cut its production.","The company reduced production, so demand was strong.","Strong demand caused production cuts."], a:1,
    hint:"\"Despite\" shows contrast. Which option still shows contrast?",
    why:"\"Although\" keeps the contrast that \"despite\" expressed. The other options turn it into cause or result."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"It rained heavily, so the event was cancelled.\" → The event was cancelled because of the heavy rain.", b:"Source: \"It rained heavily, so the event was cancelled.\" → Although it rained heavily, the event was cancelled."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing how its clauses are connected, keeping the same relationship.",
    text:"Source: \"Although online courses are flexible, many students find it difficult to stay motivated.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source by changing how its clauses are connected, keeping the same relationship.",
    text:"Source: \"The trial was stopped early because several participants experienced serious side effects.\""},
  criteria:["The clause structure is changed","The logical relationship is kept","Grammar and punctuation are accurate","The original meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"When paraphrasing a sentence with because, although or so, try expressing the same relationship with a noun phrase (due to, despite, as a result of)."
},
"para.patchwriting": {
  name:"Avoiding patchwriting", tech:"Patchwriting",
  short:"Write paraphrases that do not just swap a few words in the source's sentence pattern.",
  ex:"Changing \"important\" to \"crucial\" and keeping the rest is still too close.",
  def:"Patchwriting is a paraphrase that keeps the source's structure and much of its wording, with only some words replaced. Many universities treat it as poor source use and, in serious cases, as plagiarism.",
  why:"Teachers write \"too close to the source\" when they can lay your sentence over the original and see the same pattern.",
  good:"Source: \"Social isolation is a significant risk factor for depression in older adults.\"\nParaphrase: Older people who have little social contact are considerably more likely to become depressed.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: Social loneliness is an important risk factor for depression in elderly adults.",
  badWhy:"Only three words changed. The structure (X is a risk factor for Y in Z) is identical.",
  strategy:["Read, cover the source, and write from memory or notes.","Change at least two things: structure (subject, voice, clauses) and vocabulary.","Put your version next to the source. If you can match it phrase by phrase, rework it.","Keep technical terms, and still cite the source."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"Access to clean water remains a major challenge in many rural communities.\"",
    steps:["Notes: rural areas – clean water – still hard to get.","New subject: people in rural areas.","New verb pattern: still struggle to obtain."],
    after:"Paraphrase: In many rural areas, people still struggle to obtain clean water."},
  check:{q:"Source: \"Climate change is a major threat to global food security.\" Which version is patchwriting?",
    opts:["Climate change is a big threat to worldwide food security.","Global food supplies are increasingly at risk as the climate changes.","As the climate changes, it is becoming harder to guarantee that the world has enough food.","Food security around the world is seriously endangered by a changing climate."], a:0,
    hint:"Look for the version you could lay over the source word by word.",
    why:"It keeps the exact pattern (Climate change is a … threat to … food security) and swaps only two words."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Exercise improves memory in older adults.\" → Older people who exercise tend to remember things better.", b:"Source: \"Exercise improves memory in older adults.\" → Exercise enhances memory in elderly adults."},
  task:{instr:"This attempted paraphrase is too close to the source. Write a new paraphrase so that both the structure and the wording are your own.",
    text:"Source: \"Part-time employment can reduce the time students have available for independent study.\"\nStudent attempt: \"Part-time work can decrease the time students have available for self-study.\""},
  indep:{instr:"This attempted paraphrase is too close to the source. Write a new paraphrase so that both the structure and the wording are your own.",
    text:"Source: \"Poor housing conditions are closely linked to respiratory illness in children.\"\nStudent attempt: \"Bad housing conditions are strongly connected to breathing illness in children.\""},
  criteria:["The structure no longer mirrors the source","The wording is mostly the student's own (technical terms may stay)","The meaning is accurate","The paraphrase reads naturally"],
  transfer:"After paraphrasing, put the source and your version side by side. If you can match them phrase by phrase, change the structure."
},
"para.qualification": {
  name:"Keeping the level of certainty", tech:"Preserving qualification (hedging)",
  short:"Keep words like may, some, tend to and suggests when you paraphrase.",
  ex:"\"may reduce\" must not become \"reduces\".",
  def:"Qualifications (may, might, some, often, tend to, suggests, is associated with) show how certain or general a claim is. A paraphrase must keep the same degree of certainty and the same scope.",
  why:"Turning \"may reduce\" into \"reduces\", or \"some students\" into \"students\", misrepresents the author and overstates the evidence.",
  good:"Source: \"The findings suggest that mindfulness may reduce anxiety in some students.\"\nParaphrase: According to the results, mindfulness could lower anxiety for certain students.",
  bad:"Paraphrase: The results show that mindfulness reduces anxiety in students.",
  badWhy:"\"Suggest\" became \"show\", \"may\" disappeared and \"some\" was dropped. The claim is now much stronger than the original.",
  strategy:["Underline every hedge or limiter in the source (may, some, often, suggests, associated with).","Find an equivalent for each (may → might or could; some → certain or a number of; suggests → indicates).","Check that you have not added certainty (prove, always, all).","Check that \"associated with\" has not become \"causes\"."],
  worked:{before:"Source: \"Long working hours are associated with a higher risk of heart disease.\"",
    steps:["Hedge: \"associated with\" means linked, not a proven cause.","Keep it: \"linked to\" or \"connected with\".","Restructure: make working long hours the subject."],
    after:"Paraphrase: Working long hours has been linked to a greater likelihood of heart disease."},
  check:{q:"Source: \"Some evidence indicates that green tea might lower blood pressure.\" Which paraphrase keeps the certainty?",
    opts:["Green tea lowers blood pressure.","Research proves green tea reduces blood pressure.","There is some evidence that green tea could reduce blood pressure.","Green tea always lowers blood pressure."], a:2,
    hint:"Find the two hedges in the source. Which option keeps both?",
    why:"\"Some evidence\" and \"could\" keep both hedges. The other options make the claim certain."},
  diag:{a:"Source: \"Smartphone use may affect sleep quality in teenagers.\" → Teenagers' sleep could be influenced by phone use.", b:"Source: \"Smartphone use may affect sleep quality in teenagers.\" → Phones ruin teenagers' sleep."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source, keeping exactly the same level of certainty and scope.",
    text:"Source: \"Early intervention programs can often reduce reading difficulties in some young children.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source, keeping exactly the same level of certainty and scope.",
    text:"Source: \"The data suggest that remote work is associated with slightly higher productivity in certain industries.\""},
  criteria:["Every hedge and limiter is kept with an equivalent","No certainty or generalisation is added","Structure and wording are changed","The meaning is kept"],
  transfer:"Before you paraphrase, circle every hedge in the source. After you paraphrase, check that each one has a match in your version."
},
"para.citation": {
  name:"Citing your paraphrase", tech:"Citation and attribution",
  short:"Show whose idea it is, even when the words are your own.",
  ex:"Nguyen (2022) found that… / … (Nguyen, 2022).",
  def:"A paraphrase still uses someone else's idea, so it needs an in-text citation. You can make the author part of the sentence (author-prominent) or put the citation in brackets (information-prominent). Follow your course's style, such as APA 7 or Harvard; APA 7 is shown here.",
  why:"Without attribution, even a good paraphrase can count as plagiarism. Reporting verbs (found, argues, suggests) also show how certain the source is.",
  good:"Nguyen (2022) found that peer feedback improved students' revision skills.\nPeer feedback appears to improve revision skills (Nguyen, 2022).",
  bad:"Peer feedback improves students' revision skills. Nguyen 2022 says this.",
  badWhy:"The first sentence presents the idea as the writer's own fact, and the citation is detached and incorrectly formatted.",
  strategy:["Every paraphrased idea needs a citation, close to the idea.","Choose author-prominent (Nguyen (2022) found…) or information-prominent (… (Nguyen, 2022)).","Choose a reporting verb that matches the source's certainty (found, argues, suggests, claims).","Check your course's style guide for punctuation and page numbers."],
  worked:{before:"Idea from Patel (2020), who argues that four-day weeks may increase productivity.",
    steps:["Paraphrase the idea in your own words.","Attribute it with an author-prominent citation and the verb \"argues\".","Keep \"may\" (as \"could\") so the certainty matches."],
    after:"Patel (2020) argues that shortening the working week to four days could raise productivity."},
  check:{q:"Which in-text citation follows APA 7 style?",
    opts:["(Lee 2019)","Lee (2019) suggests that…","Lee, 2019, suggests that…","(2019, Lee)"], a:1,
    hint:"In APA 7, an author-prominent citation puts the year in brackets straight after the name.",
    why:"APA 7 author-prominent citations use Author (year). In brackets, it is (Lee, 2019), with a comma."},
  diag:{a:"(Both use Garcia's idea.) Remote learning may widen achievement gaps (Garcia, 2021).", b:"(Both use Garcia's idea.) Remote learning widens achievement gaps."},
  task:{instr:"Paraphrase the source and attribute it correctly (APA 7 unless your course uses another style). Use a reporting verb that matches the certainty.",
    text:"Source (Okafor, 2023): \"Mentoring programs may increase the confidence of first-generation university students.\""},
  indep:{instr:"Paraphrase the source and attribute it correctly. Use a reporting verb that matches the certainty.",
    text:"Source (Hughes, 2021): \"Daily physical activity is associated with improved concentration among primary school pupils.\""},
  criteria:["An in-text citation is included and correctly formatted","The reporting verb matches the source's certainty","The paraphrase is accurate and in the student's own words","It is clear which idea comes from the source"],
  transfer:"After each paraphrase in your next assignment, check: is there a citation, and does the reporting verb match how certain the source is?"
},

/* ============================== PARAGRAPH DEVELOPMENT ============================== */
"pdev.topic": {
  name:"Writing a clear topic sentence", tech:"Topic sentence and controlling idea",
  short:"Tell the reader the paragraph's main point at the start.",
  ex:"\"Public libraries support social inclusion in two key ways.\"",
  def:"A topic sentence states the paragraph's main point. It has a topic and a controlling idea: what you will say about the topic. The rest of the paragraph develops it.",
  why:"Readers use the topic sentence to predict and follow the paragraph. Without one, even good evidence seems disconnected.",
  good:"Flexible deadlines can improve the quality of student work.",
  bad:"There are many things to say about deadlines.",
  badWhy:"This names a topic but has no controlling idea, so the reader does not know what the paragraph will argue.",
  strategy:["Name the topic (flexible deadlines).","Add your controlling idea: what you claim about it (they can improve the quality of work).","Make it specific enough to develop in one paragraph, but not a single detail or statistic.","Check that every later sentence supports it."],
  worked:{before:"Social media is used by many people nowadays.",
    steps:["Topic: social media. Controlling idea: missing (this is a general fact).","Decide the paragraph's point: misleading political information spreads easily.","Combine the topic and the controlling idea."],
    after:"Social media has made it easier for misleading political information to spread."},
  check:{q:"Which is the most effective topic sentence for an academic paragraph?",
    opts:["In 2020, 62% of adults used online banking.","Online banking is a thing that exists.","Online banking has reduced costs for both customers and banks.","This paragraph will talk about banks."], a:2,
    hint:"Look for a sentence with a topic and a claim about it, not a statistic or an announcement.",
    why:"It has a topic (online banking) and a controlling idea (reduced costs for customers and banks) that a paragraph can develop."},
  diag:{a:"Urban gardens can strengthen local communities.", b:"Urban gardens are gardens in cities."},
  task:{instr:"The first sentence of this paragraph is a weak topic sentence. Write a stronger one that fits the rest of the paragraph.",
    text:"Homework is something students get. It helps them practise skills learned in class, and regular practice improves long-term memory. Homework also teaches students to manage their own time, which is essential at university."},
  indep:{instr:"The first sentence of this paragraph is a weak topic sentence. Write a stronger one that fits the rest of the paragraph.",
    text:"Electric cars are cars that use electricity. They produce no exhaust emissions, which improves air quality in cities. They are also quieter than petrol cars, reducing noise in busy areas."},
  criteria:["The topic sentence has a clear topic and controlling idea","It covers what the paragraph actually discusses","It is a claim, not a bare fact or detail","It is clear and grammatical"],
  transfer:"For each body paragraph in your next assignment, underline the topic sentence and circle its controlling idea."
},
"pdev.explain": {
  name:"Explaining your main point", tech:"Explanation",
  short:"Say what your claim means and why it is true before giving evidence.",
  ex:"\"…This is because flexible deadlines reduce pressure, allowing deeper revision.\"",
  def:"Explanation sentences usually come straight after the topic sentence. They clarify what the claim means and give the reasoning behind it.",
  why:"Readers need to understand your reasoning before evidence can support it. Paragraphs that jump from claim to statistic feel thin.",
  good:"Flexible deadlines can improve the quality of student work. When students can choose when to submit within a set window, they can plan around other commitments and spend more time revising.",
  bad:"Flexible deadlines can improve the quality of student work. A 2021 study found grades rose by 6%.",
  badWhy:"The paragraph jumps straight to evidence, with no explanation of why flexibility might improve work.",
  strategy:["After the topic sentence, ask: what do I mean, and why is it true?","Write one or two sentences answering those questions in your own words.","Use explanation language: This means…, This is because…, In other words…","Then move to evidence."],
  worked:{before:"Bilingual education benefits children. Research by Lee (2020) found higher test scores.",
    steps:["Missing: why would bilingual education help?","Reason: learning in two languages exercises attention and problem-solving.","Add an explanation sentence between the claim and the evidence."],
    after:"Bilingual education benefits children. Learning in two languages requires children to switch attention constantly, which appears to strengthen problem-solving skills. Research by Lee (2020) found higher test scores among bilingual pupils."},
  check:{q:"Which sentence best explains the claim \"Remote work can increase employee productivity\"?",
    opts:["Many companies use video calls.","Without long commutes and office interruptions, employees can focus for longer periods.","A 2022 survey of 1,000 workers was conducted.","Remote work is popular."], a:1,
    hint:"An explanation answers \"why might this be true?\"",
    why:"It gives the reasoning (fewer commutes and interruptions → longer focus). The others are facts or opinions that do not explain the claim."},
  diag:{a:"Cycling lanes improve public health. They encourage people to exercise as part of their daily travel.", b:"Cycling lanes improve public health. Copenhagen has hundreds of kilometres of them."},
  task:{instr:"Add one or two explanation sentences after the topic sentence, before the evidence.",
    text:"Free public transport could reduce traffic congestion. In one city, car traffic fell by 5% after fares were removed (Martin, 2019)."},
  indep:{instr:"Add one or two explanation sentences after the topic sentence, before the evidence.",
    text:"Learning a musical instrument may support children's academic development. In one study, children who took music lessons scored higher in mathematics (Rossi, 2020)."},
  criteria:["The explanation clarifies what the claim means","It gives a reason why the claim may be true","It sits logically between the claim and the evidence","It does not simply repeat the topic sentence"],
  transfer:"In your next paragraph, check that there is at least one \"why\" sentence between your topic sentence and your first citation."
},
"pdev.elaborate": {
  name:"Developing ideas in more depth", tech:"Elaboration",
  short:"Add detail, conditions or consequences so each idea is fully developed.",
  ex:"\"…especially for students with caring responsibilities.\"",
  def:"Elaboration expands a point by adding specific detail, conditions (when, for whom), consequences or a contrast, so the idea is developed rather than just stated.",
  why:"\"Develop your ideas\" often means your points are stated but not expanded. Elaboration shows the depth of your thinking.",
  good:"Online lectures increase access to education. In particular, students who work or live far from campus can study at times that suit them, which may reduce dropout rates.",
  bad:"Online lectures increase access to education. They are good. Many students like them.",
  badWhy:"The extra sentences add opinion, not development: nothing about who benefits, how, or with what result.",
  strategy:["Ask questions about your point: Who exactly? How? Under what conditions? With what result?","Answer one or two of them in full sentences.","Use elaboration language: In particular…, This is especially true when…, As a result…","Avoid adding new, unrelated points."],
  worked:{before:"Tourism helps local economies. It creates jobs.",
    steps:["Ask: which jobs, for whom, and what follows?","Add specific detail and a consequence."],
    after:"Tourism helps local economies. It creates jobs not only in hotels and restaurants but also in transport and local crafts, and much of the income from these jobs is spent within the community."},
  check:{q:"Which sentence best elaborates \"Recycling programs reduce waste\"?",
    opts:["Recycling is important.","In particular, collecting food scraps separately can greatly reduce the amount of household rubbish sent to landfill.","Many people recycle.","Waste is a problem."], a:1,
    hint:"Elaboration adds a specific detail about how or with what result.",
    why:"It adds specific detail (food scraps) and a consequence (less landfill). The others repeat or state general opinions."},
  diag:{a:"Exams cause stress. This is especially true when a single exam determines most of the final grade.", b:"Exams cause stress. Stress is bad."},
  task:{instr:"Elaborate on the point \"Many students experience anxiety\" with one or two sentences that say who, when, or with what result.",
    text:"Universities should offer more mental health support. Many students experience anxiety. Counselling services are available on most campuses."},
  indep:{instr:"Elaborate on the point \"Some employees feel isolated\" with one or two sentences that say who, when, or with what result.",
    text:"Working from home has disadvantages. Some employees feel isolated. Companies are trying different solutions."},
  criteria:["Adds specific detail, conditions or consequences","Stays focused on the same point","Avoids empty opinion or repetition","Is clear and grammatical"],
  transfer:"For each main point in your next paragraph, ask yourself \"who exactly, how, and so what?\" and answer at least one."
},
"pdev.evidence": {
  name:"Supporting claims with evidence", tech:"Supporting evidence",
  short:"Back up your point with relevant research, data or expert sources.",
  ex:"\"Ahmed (2021) found that students who attended… scored 8% higher.\"",
  def:"Evidence is information from credible sources (research findings, data, expert views) that supports your claim. It must be relevant, accurately reported and cited.",
  why:"In academic writing, claims without support read as opinion. Evidence that does not match the claim weakens the paragraph.",
  good:"Peer tutoring may improve exam results. In a study of 300 first-year students, those who attended peer tutoring scored 8% higher on average (Ahmed, 2021).",
  bad:"Peer tutoring may improve exam results. Everyone knows tutoring helps.",
  badWhy:"\"Everyone knows\" is not evidence. There is no source, no data and no citation.",
  strategy:["Choose evidence that supports the claim directly, not just the topic.","Introduce it: According to…, X (year) found that…","Report it accurately, keeping the source's certainty.","Cite it in your required style."],
  worked:{before:"Green roofs can reduce building energy use. My friend has one and likes it.",
    steps:["A personal anecdote is not credible academic evidence.","Use research about green roofs and energy use.","Introduce it and cite it."],
    after:"Green roofs can reduce building energy use. For example, Chen (2019) reported that buildings with green roofs needed up to 15% less energy for summer cooling."},
  check:{q:"Which is the strongest evidence for \"Sleep deprivation affects academic performance\"?",
    opts:["I always do badly when I'm tired.","Students say they feel tired.","A study of 1,200 students found lower grades among those sleeping under six hours (Park, 2022).","Sleep is very important for everyone."], a:2,
    hint:"Look for a credible, cited source that connects sleep and performance.",
    why:"It is a cited study that directly links sleep and grades. The others are anecdote, unrelated facts or opinion."},
  diag:{a:"Bike-share schemes reduce car trips. A survey of users found that 30% had replaced a car journey (Lopez, 2020).", b:"Bike-share schemes reduce car trips. Bikes are very popular these days."},
  task:{instr:"Replace the weak sentence with the most relevant piece of available evidence. Introduce it and cite it correctly.",
    text:"Paragraph: \"Group projects help students develop teamwork skills. Lots of people think group work is useful.\"\nAvailable evidence:\n(a) In a national survey, employers ranked teamwork as the most important graduate skill (Brown, 2020).\n(b) Students who completed three or more group projects rated their collaboration skills significantly higher than other students (Silva, 2022).\n(c) Group projects are common in business degrees."},
  indep:{instr:"Replace the weak sentence with the most relevant piece of available evidence. Introduce it and cite it correctly.",
    text:"Paragraph: \"Reducing class sizes can improve learning in primary schools. Small classes are nice.\"\nAvailable evidence:\n(a) Parents generally prefer smaller classes (Jones, 2019).\n(b) Pupils in classes of under 20 made faster progress in reading over two years than pupils in larger classes (Ito, 2021).\n(c) Many private schools have small classes."},
  criteria:["The evidence directly supports the claim","It is introduced smoothly","It is reported accurately and cited","Opinion or anecdote is removed"],
  transfer:"For each claim in your next assignment, ask: \"What is my evidence, and does it support this exact claim?\""
},
"pdev.examples": {
  name:"Using examples well", tech:"Examples",
  short:"Make an abstract point concrete with a specific, relevant example.",
  ex:"\"For example, students without home internet may miss online assessments.\"",
  def:"An example is a specific case that illustrates a general point. It helps readers understand, but it is not the same as research evidence, and it must be clearly linked to the point.",
  why:"Abstract points are hard to picture. A well-chosen example makes them concrete; a random one distracts.",
  good:"Some technologies can widen inequality. For example, students without reliable internet at home may be unable to access online assessments.",
  bad:"Some technologies can widen inequality. For example, smartphones have cameras.",
  badWhy:"The example is about technology, but it does not illustrate inequality.",
  strategy:["Choose a specific case that shows the general point clearly.","Introduce it: For example…, For instance…, such as…","Keep it brief and relevant.","Link it back to the point if the connection is not obvious."],
  worked:{before:"Governments can change behaviour through taxes. For example, many people drink coffee.",
    steps:["Coffee does not illustrate a tax changing behaviour.","Find a tax that changes behaviour: a tax on sugary drinks.","Introduce it and link it to the point."],
    after:"Governments can change behaviour through taxes. For example, a tax on sugary drinks can encourage manufacturers to reduce sugar and consumers to choose healthier options."},
  check:{q:"Which example best illustrates \"Some jobs are being changed by automation\"?",
    opts:["Many people like their jobs.","For instance, supermarket self-checkouts have reduced the need for cashiers.","Robots appear in films.","Automation is a modern word."], a:1,
    hint:"Which option names a specific job that automation has changed?",
    why:"It names a specific job (cashier) and a specific technology that changed it."},
  diag:{a:"Cities can reduce heat with nature. For example, street trees provide shade and cool the air.", b:"Cities can reduce heat with nature. For example, many cities have museums."},
  task:{instr:"Add a relevant example to this paragraph. Introduce it clearly and link it to the point.",
    text:"Small changes in daily routines can reduce household energy use. These changes cost little and add up over time."},
  indep:{instr:"Add a relevant example to this paragraph. Introduce it clearly and link it to the point.",
    text:"Social media can help small businesses reach new customers. This is particularly useful for businesses with limited advertising budgets."},
  criteria:["The example is specific","It clearly illustrates the paragraph's point","It is introduced appropriately","It is linked back to the point where needed"],
  transfer:"After each example in your next paragraph, ask: could a reader see immediately how this shows my point?"
},
"pdev.interpret": {
  name:"Explaining what your evidence shows", tech:"Interpretation and significance",
  short:"After evidence, explain what it shows and why it matters.",
  ex:"\"This suggests that… This is significant because…\"",
  def:"Interpretation comes after evidence. It explains what the evidence shows, how it supports your claim and why it matters. Teachers often call missing interpretation \"too descriptive\" or \"dropped evidence\".",
  why:"Evidence does not speak for itself. Your interpretation is where your own thinking appears, and it often moves a paragraph from description to analysis.",
  good:"…students in the program had a 20% lower dropout rate (Kim, 2021). This suggests that early academic support may help students adjust to university, which is significant because first-year dropout is costly for students and institutions.",
  bad:"…students in the program had a 20% lower dropout rate (Kim, 2021). Another study also looked at dropout rates.",
  badWhy:"The paragraph moves on without explaining what the 20% figure means for the claim.",
  strategy:["After each piece of evidence, write at least one sentence of interpretation.","Answer: What does this show? How does it support my claim? Why does it matter?","Use language such as: This suggests…, This means that…, This is significant because…","Keep the certainty appropriate (suggests, may)."],
  worked:{before:"Cities with congestion charges saw traffic fall by 15% (Ward, 2018). Congestion charges are used in several cities.",
    steps:["The second sentence is just more description.","What does the 15% show? Pricing changes how people drive.","Why does it matter? Less traffic also means less pollution."],
    after:"Cities with congestion charges saw traffic fall by 15% (Ward, 2018). This indicates that pricing can change how often people drive, which matters because reduced traffic also lowers air pollution."},
  check:{q:"Which sentence interprets this evidence? \"Students who used the study app spent 40% more time revising (Diaz, 2022).\"",
    opts:["The app was released in 2021.","This suggests that the app may encourage students to revise more regularly.","Diaz is a researcher.","Another app is also popular."], a:1,
    hint:"Interpretation explains what the evidence shows.",
    why:"It explains what the 40% figure suggests, with appropriate caution. The others add facts without interpreting."},
  diag:{a:"…reading for pleasure was linked to larger vocabularies (Hall, 2020). This suggests that leisure reading could support language development.", b:"…reading for pleasure was linked to larger vocabularies (Hall, 2020). Hall's study involved 500 children."},
  task:{instr:"Add one or two sentences of interpretation after the evidence: what it shows, how it supports the claim, and why it matters.",
    text:"Workplace flexibility can help retain staff. In one company survey, 65% of employees said flexible hours were a major reason for staying in their job (Reid, 2022)."},
  indep:{instr:"Add one or two sentences of interpretation after the evidence: what it shows, how it supports the claim, and why it matters.",
    text:"Free breakfast programs may improve school attendance. Schools that introduced free breakfasts recorded 10% fewer absences over one year (Morgan, 2021)."},
  criteria:["Explains what the evidence shows","Connects it clearly to the claim","Explains why it matters","Uses appropriate certainty"],
  transfer:"In your next draft, find each citation and check that the next sentence starts to explain what it shows (This suggests…, This means…)."
},
"pdev.connect": {
  name:"Linking back to your argument", tech:"Concluding or linking sentence",
  short:"Finish the paragraph by connecting it to your thesis or the essay question.",
  ex:"\"For this reason, flexible assessment should be considered…\"",
  def:"A linking or concluding sentence shows how the paragraph's point supports your overall argument (thesis) or the essay question. It can also prepare the reader for the next paragraph.",
  why:"Without a link, the reader has to work out why the paragraph matters for the essay. Teachers may write \"link to your argument\" or \"so what?\".",
  good:"(Essay: Should universities replace exams with coursework?)\n…Therefore, because coursework assesses skills over time, it offers a fairer picture of student ability than a single exam.",
  bad:"…Therefore, coursework is interesting.",
  badWhy:"This sentence does not connect to the essay's argument about fairness or replacing exams.",
  strategy:["Re-read your thesis or the essay question.","State how this paragraph's point supports it.","Use language such as: Therefore…, For this reason…, This shows that…","Do not repeat the topic sentence word for word or add a new point."],
  worked:{before:"(Thesis: Cities should invest more in public transport.)\n…Buses reduce the number of cars on the road. So buses are good.",
    steps:["\"Buses are good\" is vague and does not mention investment.","Link the point to the thesis: investment → fewer cars → less congestion."],
    after:"…For this reason, increased investment in bus networks would help cities reduce congestion."},
  check:{q:"Thesis: \"Governments should fund free language classes for migrants.\" Which sentence best concludes a paragraph about employment?",
    opts:["Jobs are important.","Therefore, funding language classes could help migrants find work more quickly, benefiting the wider economy.","Many migrants live in cities.","Language is complex."], a:1,
    hint:"Which option mentions both the paragraph's point (employment) and the thesis (funding classes)?",
    why:"It links the paragraph's point (finding work) back to the thesis (funding language classes)."},
  diag:{a:"(Thesis: schools should teach financial literacy.) …Thus, teaching budgeting at school would prepare students for adult life.", b:"(Thesis: schools should teach financial literacy.) …Thus, money is important."},
  task:{instr:"Write a concluding sentence that links this paragraph to the thesis.",
    text:"Thesis: Universities should offer more online courses.\nParagraph: Online courses allow students to study at their own pace. Students can re-watch lectures on difficult topics and fit study around work or family commitments. This flexibility is particularly valuable for mature-age students."},
  indep:{instr:"Write a concluding sentence that links this paragraph to the thesis.",
    text:"Thesis: Cities should ban cars from their central business districts.\nParagraph: Car-free zones make city centres safer for pedestrians. Without traffic, accidents involving pedestrians fall, and families feel more comfortable visiting shops and cafés."},
  criteria:["Clearly connects to the thesis","Sums up the paragraph's contribution without copying it","Adds no new, unsupported point","Is clear and grammatical"],
  transfer:"Read only your thesis and the last sentence of each body paragraph. Each last sentence should show how that paragraph supports the thesis."
},
"pdev.unity": {
  name:"Keeping to one main idea", tech:"Paragraph unity",
  short:"Remove sentences that do not support the paragraph's main point.",
  ex:"A paragraph about costs should not suddenly discuss the weather.",
  def:"A unified paragraph develops one main idea. Every sentence supports the topic sentence; points that belong elsewhere are moved or removed.",
  why:"Off-topic sentences distract the reader and weaken the argument. Teachers may write \"irrelevant\" or \"stay focused\".",
  good:"Studying abroad develops independence. Students must manage money, housing and daily tasks alone, and they learn to solve problems without family nearby.",
  bad:"Studying abroad develops independence. Students must manage money and housing alone. Australia has many beautiful beaches. They also learn to solve problems.",
  badWhy:"The sentence about beaches has nothing to do with independence.",
  strategy:["Underline the controlling idea in your topic sentence.","For each sentence, ask: does this support that idea?","Remove or move sentences that do not.","If a sentence is related but different, it may need its own paragraph."],
  worked:{before:"Electric scooters can reduce short car trips. Many commuters use them for distances under 3 km. Scooters come in many colours. They are also cheaper than taxis.",
    steps:["Controlling idea: reducing short car trips.","\"Many colours\" is irrelevant: remove it.","\"Cheaper than taxis\" is about cost, not car trips: remove it or move it to another paragraph."],
    after:"Electric scooters can reduce short car trips. Many commuters use them instead of driving for distances under 3 km, particularly to reach train stations."},
  check:{q:"Topic sentence: \"Regular exercise improves mental health.\" Which sentence does NOT belong?",
    opts:["Physical activity releases chemicals that improve mood.","People who exercise report lower levels of anxiety.","Gym memberships are increasing in price.","Even short walks can reduce feelings of stress."], a:2,
    hint:"Which sentence is about something other than mental health?",
    why:"Gym prices are about cost, not mental health, so that sentence does not support the topic sentence."},
  diag:{a:"Urban farms improve food security. They supply fresh produce locally and reduce dependence on long supply chains.", b:"Urban farms improve food security. They supply fresh produce locally. Farming began about 10,000 years ago."},
  task:{instr:"Make this paragraph unified: remove or change the sentences that do not support the topic sentence.",
    text:"Reading fiction can develop empathy. When readers follow a character's thoughts, they practise seeing the world from another perspective. E-books are cheaper than printed books. Some studies have linked regular fiction reading with a better understanding of others' emotions. Libraries are open on weekends."},
  indep:{instr:"Make this paragraph unified: remove or change the sentences that do not support the topic sentence.",
    text:"Learning a second language improves career opportunities. Many international companies prefer bilingual employees. Spanish is spoken in over 20 countries. Language skills can also lead to roles in translation, diplomacy and tourism. Grammar can be difficult to learn."},
  criteria:["Every sentence supports the controlling idea","Irrelevant sentences are removed","The paragraph still flows","The remaining content keeps its meaning"],
  transfer:"Before submitting, read each body paragraph and ask of every sentence: does this support my topic sentence?"
}
};
